WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:07.920
Imagine for a second that everything you know about reality, I mean, about intelligence, even about your own memory, is just completely backwards.

00:08.220 --> 00:09.680
Yeah, totally upside down.

00:09.780 --> 00:12.100
Right. Think about how we usually define things.

00:12.220 --> 00:17.520
You know, we look at an object or a person or a state of mind and we ask, well, what is this right now?

00:17.660 --> 00:18.880
Exactly. We take a snapshot.

00:19.140 --> 00:19.700
Yeah, a snapshot.

00:20.100 --> 00:23.460
We define reality by what currently exists in front of us.

00:23.960 --> 00:29.060
But what if a thing isn't defined by what it is in the present moment?

00:29.060 --> 00:31.740
Which is a huge mental leap to make.

00:31.920 --> 00:37.180
It really is. What if instead a thing is defined entirely by what it can become next?

00:37.920 --> 00:43.100
Today, we are ripping apart a 2026 monograph by an independent researcher named Flyxion.

00:43.800 --> 00:47.700
And, well, it forces us to completely invert our view of reality.

00:47.820 --> 00:53.420
It really does. The paper is called Continuations Before Objects, A Geometric Theory of Intelligent Persistence.

00:53.440 --> 00:54.440
Such a dense title.

00:54.440 --> 01:02.080
Oh, absolutely. But it is honestly one of the most mathematically rigorous, just mind-bending pieces of architecture I have read in a very long time.

01:02.160 --> 01:03.900
It really is a total ontological inversion.

01:04.260 --> 01:07.400
Yeah, that's the perfect way to put it. The paradigm shift Flyxion is proposing.

01:07.760 --> 01:15.160
It challenges the foundational building blocks of how we understand computation, cognition, and honestly, the passage of time itself.

01:15.620 --> 01:15.860
Wow.

01:15.860 --> 01:19.340
Yeah, it's a complete teardown of the modern technological stack.

01:19.720 --> 01:27.060
And when we say it challenges everything, we mean it goes after the very architecture of modern artificial intelligence.

01:27.180 --> 01:28.300
Right, the hardware too.

01:28.420 --> 01:36.020
Yeah, the way we physically manufacture computer chips and even how we structure human society and measure success.

01:36.140 --> 01:37.280
Which gets pretty intense.

01:37.420 --> 01:37.840
It does.

01:37.840 --> 01:45.160
Yes. So, our mission today for this deep dive is to unpack what Flyxion calls the admissibility program.

01:45.660 --> 01:52.800
Right. The core argument here is that modern AI, cognitive science, and even our societal systems are fundamentally failing.

01:53.020 --> 01:55.440
Failing because they optimize for static states, right?

01:55.440 --> 01:58.320
Exactly. They optimize for states and they optimize for predictions.

01:58.560 --> 02:05.160
And we are going to guide you through the shift from that static worldview to what the paper calls a reachability-first worldview.

02:05.300 --> 02:06.580
Which applies to literally everything.

02:06.580 --> 02:07.940
Yeah, it really does.

02:08.280 --> 02:16.300
We'll explore how this applies to the underlying mechanics of how a swipe keyboard works on your smartphone, the physical nature of human memory.

02:16.480 --> 02:17.520
That part blew my mind.

02:17.600 --> 02:18.320
Oh, it's incredible.

02:18.480 --> 02:25.100
And we'll get into the mathematical collapse of meritocracy and even the design of clockless silicon computer chips.

02:25.540 --> 02:26.920
Okay, so let's unpack this.

02:27.300 --> 02:35.760
Because I promised you that while the math in this text is incredibly dense, the takeaways are going to completely change how you view your own daily choices.

02:35.760 --> 02:36.340
Absolutely.

02:37.160 --> 02:44.860
So to understand the solution Flyxion is proposing, we really have to look at the massive target they painted on the back of current technology.

02:44.980 --> 02:45.760
Right, the diagnosis.

02:46.060 --> 02:46.220
Yeah.

02:46.900 --> 02:55.260
Flyxion opens the monograph by diagnosing a fatal flaw in what they call prediction primary architectures.

02:55.580 --> 02:58.480
Which, to be clear, is essentially everything we build today.

02:58.620 --> 02:58.980
Everything.

02:58.980 --> 03:00.860
Look at the dominant paradigms.

03:00.960 --> 03:10.120
You have the von Neumann architecture in our hardware, large language models generating text, reinforcement learning agents, beating humans at chess or, you know, video games.

03:10.120 --> 03:14.500
All of these systems are entirely based on predicting the next optimal state.

03:15.200 --> 03:19.660
The system evaluates itself based on how accurately it can guess what happens next.

03:19.800 --> 03:21.820
Or what action will give it the highest reward.

03:21.820 --> 03:22.440
Exactly.

03:22.800 --> 03:29.360
If the guess aligns with the training data, the internal representation is locked in and considered optimal.

03:29.880 --> 03:33.420
And the technical term for this, right, is minimizing prediction loss.

03:33.560 --> 03:34.160
That's the one.

03:34.160 --> 03:46.400
The AI builds a mathematical model of the world, and if that model accurately predicts the next frame of a video or, say, the next word in a sentence, the model's structure is rewarded.

03:46.620 --> 03:53.140
But here's where Flyxion comes in with this devastating mathematical critique called the theorem of prediction collapse.

03:53.300 --> 03:54.620
This is where things get wild.

03:54.620 --> 04:08.080
This theorem mathematically proves that if two different states of the world produce the exact same optimal action right now, a prediction-first system will inevitably collapse them into the exact same internal representation.

04:08.480 --> 04:09.720
It just squashes them together.

04:09.860 --> 04:10.200
Literally.

04:10.420 --> 04:11.820
It cannot tell them apart anymore.

04:12.120 --> 04:17.580
It becomes completely blind to any future possibilities that it isn't currently trying to predict.

04:17.580 --> 04:29.580
I have to pause you there because my immediate instinct, and probably the instinct of anyone who works in tech, is to ask, wait, isn't predicting the future the whole point of intelligence?

04:29.880 --> 04:30.820
It seems like it, right.

04:30.940 --> 04:31.100
Yeah.

04:31.280 --> 04:40.520
Like, if a system can predict exactly what's going to happen next and execute the perfect optimal action, isn't that literally the definition of a smart system?

04:40.720 --> 04:42.160
That is definitely the dogma.

04:42.460 --> 04:46.400
Okay, let me try an analogy to see if I'm grasping why this is actually a flaw.

04:46.400 --> 04:50.380
Let's say I am trying to predict my friend's daily commute.

04:50.660 --> 04:51.580
Okay, commute analogy.

04:51.740 --> 04:52.180
I like it.

04:52.440 --> 04:59.460
So, I observe them pulling out of their driveway and taking this highly specific, really convoluted route to work every single day.

04:59.580 --> 04:59.820
Right.

05:00.220 --> 05:04.780
My prediction model maps this out and says, okay, this person is a commuter on route A.

05:05.160 --> 05:05.820
Simple enough.

05:06.500 --> 05:09.220
Now, suppose another friend drives that exact same route.

05:09.980 --> 05:13.080
My prediction system categorizes them identically, right?

05:13.420 --> 05:15.300
They're both commuter on route A.

05:15.300 --> 05:20.680
Because the immediate action, you know, driving down that specific sequence of streets, is identical in both cases.

05:20.820 --> 05:22.560
Your model's prediction loss is zero.

05:23.100 --> 05:24.560
The system is perfectly satisfied.

05:25.180 --> 05:27.680
But the model is completely ignoring the why.

05:28.260 --> 05:30.700
And, more importantly, the what next.

05:30.940 --> 05:31.340
Exactly.

05:31.340 --> 05:35.320
What if friend one takes that route purely out of blind habit?

05:35.880 --> 05:38.020
Like, it's just the way their parents taught them to drive.

05:38.180 --> 05:38.320
Right.

05:38.440 --> 05:39.360
No deeper reason.

05:39.560 --> 05:39.720
Yeah.

05:40.140 --> 05:45.680
But friend two takes that exact same route because it happens to pass right by a specific hospital.

05:45.940 --> 05:47.680
And they have a pregnant partner at home.

05:48.140 --> 05:54.740
So, they've meticulously chosen a path that ensures they are never more than a three-minute detour from an emergency room.

05:54.980 --> 05:56.560
Oh, that's a brilliant distinction.

05:56.560 --> 05:56.960
Right.

05:57.180 --> 06:01.040
So, their current state and their current action are absolutely identical.

06:02.000 --> 06:05.460
A prediction system says they are the exact same entity in that moment.

06:05.600 --> 06:08.060
But their future potential is vastly different.

06:08.200 --> 06:08.640
Exactly.

06:08.940 --> 06:11.500
The menu of paths they might suddenly need to take.

06:12.180 --> 06:17.040
Friend two has a highly reachable future that involves a sudden left turn into a triage bay.

06:17.200 --> 06:19.680
And friend one doesn't even know the hospital exists.

06:20.160 --> 06:23.080
That perfectly illustrates the mechanism of prediction collapse.

06:23.080 --> 06:36.880
By optimizing strictly for the prediction of the current optimal action, driving straight down the road, the mathematical system actively deletes any internal distinction that doesn't immediately serve that specific prediction.

06:37.080 --> 06:38.160
It just wipes it out.

06:38.240 --> 06:40.120
It strips away all the latent variables.

06:40.420 --> 06:43.640
It literally destroys the differences in their future trajectories.

06:43.640 --> 06:49.340
And what's fascinating here is that Flyxion is not the first person to notice this.

06:49.820 --> 06:57.040
Yeah, the monograph points out that this exact flaw was discovered independently across four completely different research disciplines.

06:57.260 --> 06:59.980
They all hit the exact same invisible wall.

07:00.220 --> 07:03.080
This was one of the most compelling parts of the introduction for me.

07:03.580 --> 07:12.420
Four different fields, totally isolated from one another, all realizing that optimizing for prediction destroys the very intelligence they were trying to build.

07:12.420 --> 07:13.360
Yeah, it's wild.

07:13.500 --> 07:16.080
Let's walk through these because it really grounds the math in reality.

07:16.500 --> 07:18.780
The first one Flyxion mentions is person.

07:19.100 --> 07:19.720
Right, person.

07:20.040 --> 07:21.420
This deals with recommendation systems.

07:22.280 --> 07:25.120
Like YouTube algorithms or Amazon shopping.

07:25.540 --> 07:26.060
Exactly.

07:26.460 --> 07:30.520
The algorithms that decide what video you watch next or what product you buy.

07:31.040 --> 07:38.560
Researchers in this field found that if you optimize a system to simply predict what a user will click on next across a wide variety of contexts,

07:39.080 --> 07:41.780
the system mathematically averages out their preferences.

07:41.780 --> 07:42.800
It makes them generic.

07:42.860 --> 07:43.320
Completely.

07:43.920 --> 07:52.040
Imagine a user who occasionally buys highly specialized niche technical books, but mostly buys everyday household items.

07:52.320 --> 07:52.540
Okay.

07:53.000 --> 08:01.480
A strict prediction system will collapse their profile into just a buyer of generic goods because that minimizes the overall prediction error.

08:01.700 --> 08:01.980
Wow.

08:02.200 --> 08:06.060
It destroys the user's specific personalized trajectory through the data.

08:06.400 --> 08:10.700
It collapses their complex future dynamics into a flat, predictable average.

08:10.700 --> 08:14.580
So the algorithm literally makes them boring because boring is easier to predict.

08:14.960 --> 08:15.340
Precisely.

08:15.460 --> 08:16.220
That is terrifying.

08:16.560 --> 08:20.560
Okay, then there's the second framework, relevance activation theory, or RIT.

08:20.900 --> 08:21.200
Yes.

08:21.560 --> 08:24.060
RIT comes from embodied cognition.

08:24.360 --> 08:27.780
Which is the study of how physical bodies interact with the mind, right?

08:27.840 --> 08:28.100
Yeah.

08:28.100 --> 08:36.780
In embodied cognition, researchers realized that human behavior isn't about picking options from a static drop-down menu in our brains.

08:36.960 --> 08:37.100
Right.

08:37.280 --> 08:38.120
We aren't computers.

08:38.280 --> 08:38.700
Exactly.

08:38.900 --> 08:42.080
It's about a fluid motion through a physical landscape of cues.

08:42.080 --> 08:51.560
The RIT framework demonstrated that a prediction map based on static states completely fails to capture the curvature of how an agent actually moves.

08:51.800 --> 08:53.120
Give me an example of that curvature.

08:53.620 --> 08:59.680
So if I place a cup of coffee on a table, a prediction AI calculates the optimal vector for my hand to grab it.

08:59.800 --> 09:00.840
Shortest distance, boom.

09:00.940 --> 09:01.060
Right.

09:01.500 --> 09:02.840
But a human doesn't do that.

09:03.180 --> 09:09.180
A human considers the heat of the cup, the precariousness of the table, the social context of who is watching them.

09:09.300 --> 09:10.860
Like, am I going to spill this on my boss?

09:10.860 --> 09:11.580
Exactly.

09:12.460 --> 09:14.880
Human behavior is a navigation of relevance.

09:15.260 --> 09:22.940
It's about maintaining options, whereas the AI simply collapses all that rich context into a single point-to-point prediction vector.

09:23.100 --> 09:24.320
It loses all the nuance.

09:24.960 --> 09:28.700
Okay, the third framework really blew my mind, the chain of memory framework.

09:28.800 --> 09:31.760
Oh, this one is incredibly dangerous for modern AI.

09:31.900 --> 09:35.080
Because it deals with causal traceability inside neural networks, right?

09:35.140 --> 09:35.280
Yeah.

09:35.280 --> 09:35.760
Yeah.

09:35.760 --> 09:42.120
The researchers found that a prediction primary AI can give you the right answer for totally wrong reasons.

09:42.260 --> 09:43.440
Which is a huge problem.

09:43.620 --> 09:44.740
A massive problem.

09:45.300 --> 09:49.840
Causal structure and predictive structure are fundamentally different mathematical beasts.

09:50.480 --> 09:54.220
A machine learning model can memorize a spurious correlation.

09:54.720 --> 09:55.120
Like what?

09:55.120 --> 10:00.800
Like noticing that every picture of a dog and its training data happens to have green grass in the background.

10:01.000 --> 10:01.560
Oh, wow.

10:01.780 --> 10:04.440
So it thinks green grass equals dog.

10:04.580 --> 10:05.020
Exactly.

10:05.440 --> 10:06.920
It predicts dog perfectly.

10:07.180 --> 10:13.160
But when the underlying rules change, or you show it a picture of a dog on a snowy field, it fails catastrophically.

10:13.160 --> 10:15.720
Because it wasn't looking for a dog, it was looking for grass.

10:15.780 --> 10:16.020
Right.

10:16.380 --> 10:26.700
Because the optimization process collapsed the complex causal history of what actually makes a dog a dog into a single, fragile, predictive weight tied to the color green.

10:26.900 --> 10:30.020
It burned down the causal bridge just to get the prediction trophy.

10:30.120 --> 10:31.600
That is exactly what happens.

10:31.800 --> 10:38.300
And the fourth field Flyxion sites is RSVP, which stands for Relativistic Scalar Vector Plenum.

10:38.680 --> 10:39.700
Yeah, quite a mouthful.

10:39.940 --> 10:41.740
This comes from field theoretic semantics.

10:41.740 --> 10:43.960
Which is about how meaning works in language.

10:44.160 --> 10:44.400
Yes.

10:44.980 --> 10:48.600
Researchers were trying to map how meaning actually works in thought.

10:49.100 --> 10:52.940
They found that the meaning of a concept is not a static representation.

10:53.560 --> 10:56.360
The meaning of a word isn't what the word is.

10:56.800 --> 11:00.940
The meaning is the space of future representations it can reach.

11:01.140 --> 11:01.900
I love that.

11:02.060 --> 11:02.240
Yeah.

11:02.360 --> 11:09.260
So if you optimize a language model just to predict the next token, you are severing the word from its space of future potential.

11:09.260 --> 11:17.740
So we have recommendation algorithms, cognitive science, AI safety researchers, and theoretical linguists all screaming the exact same thing.

11:17.800 --> 11:18.960
Which is rare, by the way.

11:19.120 --> 11:19.920
Very rare.

11:20.020 --> 11:20.180
Yeah.

11:20.260 --> 11:25.300
But they all agree that prediction first optimization destroys future relevant distinctions.

11:25.640 --> 11:26.980
It makes systems brittle.

11:27.140 --> 11:27.760
So brittle.

11:27.760 --> 11:32.540
So if prediction is a totally flawed foundation, what do we replace it with?

11:32.740 --> 11:35.720
Like how do we build an intelligence that doesn't collapse the future?

11:35.920 --> 11:38.440
This is where Flyxion introduces the reachability turn.

11:38.580 --> 11:39.560
The reachability turn.

11:39.600 --> 11:39.840
Yes.

11:40.220 --> 11:43.040
If we abandon prediction, we replace it with reachability.

11:43.620 --> 11:48.260
And executing that turn requires three massive ontological inversions.

11:48.260 --> 11:51.740
We have to shift our fundamental units of measurement.

11:52.040 --> 11:52.120
Right.

11:52.560 --> 11:54.720
First, we shift from states to trajectories.

11:55.160 --> 11:58.480
Second, we shift from representations to continuations.

11:58.740 --> 12:03.240
And third, we shift from optimization to admissibility.

12:03.500 --> 12:08.600
Let's take those one by one because they sound academic, but they are incredibly radical in practice.

12:08.620 --> 12:08.880
Yes.

12:09.040 --> 12:09.880
Completely radical.

12:10.120 --> 12:11.780
Shifting from states to trajectories.

12:11.960 --> 12:15.200
Normally, we think of a trajectory as just a sequence of states.

12:15.380 --> 12:15.460
Yeah.

12:15.500 --> 12:16.480
Like connect the dots.

12:16.620 --> 12:16.940
Exactly.

12:16.940 --> 12:22.220
We have state A, then state B, then state C, and the line connecting them is the trajectory.

12:22.840 --> 12:25.280
But Flyxion says, flip that upside down.

12:25.660 --> 12:29.580
The trajectory is the primitive, fundamental object of reality.

12:30.100 --> 12:35.000
The state is just a momentary artificial coordinate chart that we slap onto that trajectory.

12:35.260 --> 12:38.720
It is a total reversal of how we do physics and computer science.

12:39.200 --> 12:44.800
In the reachability framework, what a state is, is determined entirely by where it came from and where it can go next.

12:44.800 --> 12:47.820
Which brings us to what Flyxion calls the continuation principle.

12:47.960 --> 12:50.380
The bedrock axiom of the entire monograph.

12:50.380 --> 13:00.740
The continuation principle dictates that two states are semantically equivalent, if and only if they induce the identical structure of admissible futures.

13:00.740 --> 13:03.160
Let me say that again, because it's so important.

13:03.660 --> 13:06.080
Meaning is not a property of the state itself.

13:06.520 --> 13:10.900
Meaning is a property of the future paths that the state makes possible.

13:10.900 --> 13:17.400
I spent hours pondering how this applies to human life, and it is profoundly confronting.

13:17.660 --> 13:18.640
Really hits home.

13:18.800 --> 13:21.160
We constantly define ourselves by our current state.

13:21.260 --> 13:26.260
I'll say, well, I'm a host, or I am a person with this much money in the bank, or I live in this specific city.

13:26.540 --> 13:29.400
We treat those static snapshots as objects.

13:29.540 --> 13:30.820
Yeah, as our core identity.

13:31.180 --> 13:31.360
Yeah.

13:31.360 --> 13:38.200
But Flyxion's continuation principle suggests that our identity, our actual semantic meaning in the universe, isn't that snapshot.

13:38.460 --> 13:38.880
Not at all.

13:38.960 --> 13:43.320
We're actually defined by the menu of futures we can access from this exact moment.

13:43.560 --> 13:43.800
Right.

13:44.060 --> 13:47.960
Let's look at two people with the exact same job title and the exact same salary.

13:48.440 --> 13:51.500
In a state-based worldview, those two people are identical.

13:52.120 --> 13:54.820
They occupy the same exact node on the corporate graph.

13:54.880 --> 13:55.840
Yep, completely identical.

13:55.840 --> 14:03.840
But if one of those people has a deep network of supportive mentors, cross-disciplinary skills, and a flexible mindset...

14:04.620 --> 14:10.060
And the other person is completely isolated and highly specialized in a dying software language.

14:10.200 --> 14:10.560
Exactly.

14:11.260 --> 14:13.580
Their admissible futures are wildly different.

14:13.800 --> 14:17.240
According to Flyxion, their current reality is entirely different.

14:17.660 --> 14:19.060
The state is an illusion.

14:19.580 --> 14:22.300
And if we connect this to the broader psychological picture,

14:22.300 --> 14:26.980
it mathematically explains why feeling stuck in life is so agonizing.

14:27.120 --> 14:28.020
Oh, that makes so much sense.

14:28.300 --> 14:28.500
Right.

14:28.820 --> 14:32.360
It's rarely because your current state is objectively torturous.

14:32.580 --> 14:35.160
You might have a comfortable house and a steady income.

14:35.460 --> 14:39.960
The agony comes from the fact that your space of admissible continuations has collapsed.

14:40.160 --> 14:42.000
Your menu of futures shrank to zero.

14:42.180 --> 14:42.560
Yes.

14:42.800 --> 14:46.580
You are experiencing a sudden, drastic loss of semantic volume.

14:47.040 --> 14:49.140
Your future trajectory has flatlined.

14:49.300 --> 14:49.540
Wow.

14:49.640 --> 14:51.920
And that perfectly sets up the third inversion, right?

14:51.920 --> 14:54.060
Shifting from optimization to admissibility.

14:54.200 --> 14:54.480
Exactly.

14:54.680 --> 14:56.180
Instead of a system constantly asking,

14:56.440 --> 14:59.900
what is the best single move to make right now to maximize my reward?

15:00.180 --> 15:01.560
An intelligence system should ask,

15:01.900 --> 15:06.440
which moves preserve the largest, richest set of future options?

15:06.780 --> 15:08.380
Admissibility must come first.

15:08.920 --> 15:12.820
You only optimize within an already safeguarded admissible space.

15:12.820 --> 15:23.480
It's the difference between playing chess to capture a pawn right now versus playing chess to control the center of the board so you have 20 different attack vectors five moves from now.

15:23.580 --> 15:25.040
That is the perfect analogy.

15:25.580 --> 15:28.040
But, I mean, this is all philosophy so far.

15:28.260 --> 15:31.980
To move from a philosophical critique to actual computer science,

15:32.560 --> 15:39.020
Flyxion introduces a blueprint for an architecture that can literally compute these futures rather than just guessing them.

15:39.020 --> 15:39.400
Right.

15:39.760 --> 15:42.860
Because if you agree that the future trajectory is what matters,

15:43.360 --> 15:46.440
the engineering question becomes, how do you store a trajectory?

15:46.660 --> 15:46.800
Right.

15:46.920 --> 15:48.200
How do you put that on a hard drive?

15:48.340 --> 15:53.420
How do you compress infinite future possibility into a finite computer system without collapsing it?

15:53.660 --> 15:56.320
Flyxion answers this with the generative compression hypothesis.

15:56.640 --> 15:56.820
Okay.

15:56.900 --> 15:57.400
Unpack that.

15:57.400 --> 16:04.500
The question is, what is the absolutely smallest structure needed to preserve the largest space of future distinctions?

16:05.180 --> 16:08.860
And the answer is that you do not preserve the inventory of current states.

16:09.220 --> 16:11.400
You preserve the minimal generator.

16:11.640 --> 16:15.160
In the mathematical notation of the paper, they use G-star for the generator, right?

16:15.200 --> 16:15.460
Yes.

16:15.820 --> 16:18.160
G-star for the generator and I for the inventory.

16:18.760 --> 16:23.480
And here is where Flyxion uses an example that made the math instantly click for me.

16:23.920 --> 16:25.640
They call it the eight-letter keyboard.

16:25.860 --> 16:26.940
I love this example.

16:26.940 --> 16:29.620
Think about how a highly skilled typist works.

16:30.160 --> 16:34.980
Or even better, think about how you type on a smartphone using a swipe keyboard.

16:35.060 --> 16:37.240
Yeah, where you drag your thumb across the glass.

16:37.400 --> 16:39.660
Right, in a continuous motion to form words.

16:39.820 --> 16:40.040
Yeah.

16:40.480 --> 16:46.600
When you are swiping quickly, you are not actually thinking about the inventory of 26 static letters printed on the screen.

16:46.700 --> 16:47.200
Not at all.

16:47.340 --> 16:51.980
If you are swiping the word hello, you aren't mentally plotting a discrete sequence.

16:52.480 --> 16:55.700
Like H-stop, E-stop, L-stop.

16:55.700 --> 16:59.440
No, you are executing a continuous motor program.

16:59.560 --> 17:01.760
It is a physical trajectory through space.

17:02.020 --> 17:02.420
Exactly.

17:02.700 --> 17:04.900
You remember a generator of loaded trajectories?

17:05.120 --> 17:10.080
The physical path your thumb takes is the primitive fundamental element of your memory.

17:10.080 --> 17:16.540
The word that pops up on the screen, the inventory, is just a derived output of that continuous gesture.

17:16.540 --> 17:19.900
You memorized the shape of the word, not the letters.

17:20.160 --> 17:20.240
Yeah.

17:20.400 --> 17:25.400
And Flyxion uses this to expose the massive vulnerability in how we currently build AI.

17:25.860 --> 17:30.540
Our naive human instinct is always to preserve the inventory, the eye.

17:30.780 --> 17:31.820
Because it's easy to see.

17:31.920 --> 17:32.100
Right.

17:32.160 --> 17:33.620
The inventory is highly visible.

17:33.860 --> 17:34.820
It feels complete.

17:35.020 --> 17:36.540
It's easy to write down in a database.

17:36.860 --> 17:37.080
Yeah.

17:37.080 --> 17:38.920
We want to catalog the 26 letters.

17:39.180 --> 17:43.000
But mathematically, losing the generator, G star, is catastrophic.

17:43.320 --> 17:46.220
Whereas losing the inventory is totally recoverable.

17:46.400 --> 17:47.560
Walk us through why that is.

17:48.080 --> 17:48.320
Okay.

17:48.440 --> 17:54.860
Imagine you somehow contracted a hyperspecific amnesia where you forgot the visual inventory of the English alphabet.

17:55.100 --> 17:55.380
Okay.

17:55.380 --> 17:57.320
You look at a keyboard and the keys are blank.

17:57.620 --> 18:01.120
But you kept your motor generator, your muscle memory of the physical paths.

18:01.260 --> 18:01.480
Right.

18:01.700 --> 18:07.460
You could sit at that blank keyboard, place your hands on the home row, and type out a perfectly coherent grammatical essay.

18:07.700 --> 18:08.620
This is off muscle memory.

18:08.760 --> 18:09.200
Exactly.

18:09.840 --> 18:13.260
The generator naturally rebuilds the inventory as you move.

18:13.680 --> 18:14.960
The reachability is preserved.

18:15.180 --> 18:15.320
Oh.

18:15.880 --> 18:17.260
But reverse the scenario.

18:17.880 --> 18:20.380
Imagine you perfectly retain the visual inventory.

18:20.380 --> 18:26.140
You can stare at the 26 letters and name them all, but you completely lose the motor generator.

18:26.580 --> 18:27.760
Your muscle memory is wiped.

18:28.080 --> 18:28.440
Wiped.

18:28.600 --> 18:32.940
You are reduced to hunting and pecking like someone who has never seen a computer before.

18:33.200 --> 18:35.200
Your speed drops to zero.

18:35.940 --> 18:43.200
The inventory without the underlying generator is incredibly brittle and basically useless for complex continuation.

18:43.760 --> 18:48.320
It's like having a massive library of books but suddenly forgetting the cognitive process of reading.

18:48.460 --> 18:48.780
Exactly.

18:48.780 --> 18:54.120
The inventory is perfectly preserved on the shelves, but the generator that gives it reachability is destroyed.

18:54.480 --> 19:00.180
So how does Flyxion map this preserve the generator concept onto an actual computer architecture?

19:00.480 --> 19:02.940
They propose a system called HiYDRA, right?

19:03.300 --> 19:03.660
Yes.

19:03.900 --> 19:06.740
HiYDRA stands for hybrid dynamic reasoning architecture.

19:07.060 --> 19:12.040
And the most important thing to grasp about HiYDRA is that it is not a sequential data pipeline.

19:12.040 --> 19:18.620
In a traditional pipeline, you just push static chunks of data from one module to the next, like a factory assembly line.

19:19.620 --> 19:20.760
HiYDRA is different.

19:21.100 --> 19:23.460
It is a chain of mathematical projections.

19:24.020 --> 19:24.060
Okay.

19:24.360 --> 19:26.600
It consists of six distinct stages.

19:27.200 --> 19:33.860
And at every single stage, the system aggressively filters out any data that does not actively preserve future reachability.

19:33.860 --> 19:38.800
It is constantly auditing the data to see if the GSTAR generator is surviving.

19:39.500 --> 19:46.500
Let's break down those six stages of HiYDRA because it really paints a clear picture of how reachability first mind would actually operate.

19:46.680 --> 19:46.900
Sure.

19:47.060 --> 19:48.660
So the first stage is recognition.

19:49.460 --> 19:53.680
Now, a traditional AI looks at an image and tries to label every single pixel.

19:54.140 --> 19:54.600
Dog tree.

19:54.820 --> 19:55.060
Cloud.

19:55.060 --> 19:55.500
Right.

19:56.080 --> 19:57.280
HYDRA doesn't do that.

19:57.380 --> 19:58.440
The world is far too noisy.

19:58.940 --> 20:03.440
HYDRA asks, which specific distinctions in this environment actually deserve attention?

20:03.600 --> 20:03.820
Okay.

20:04.140 --> 20:09.860
It creates an activated subspace, filtering only for features that might actively participate in a future trajectory.

20:10.200 --> 20:15.600
So it ignores the static background entirely and only highlights the objects it can interact with or that might move.

20:15.640 --> 20:16.040
Exactly.

20:16.140 --> 20:16.900
What's stage two?

20:17.140 --> 20:19.180
Stage two is graph construction.

20:19.180 --> 20:25.460
Instead of building a static knowledge graph of disconnected facts, it asks, where can I still go?

20:25.840 --> 20:30.160
It takes those active features from stage one and maps out possible transitions.

20:30.520 --> 20:32.220
It builds a map of affordances.

20:32.760 --> 20:32.900
Okay.

20:33.020 --> 20:35.140
Stage three seems to be the most critical one.

20:35.760 --> 20:36.820
Admissibility filtering.

20:37.680 --> 20:40.860
This is the absolute heart of HYDRA.

20:41.300 --> 20:48.780
The system looks at that sprawling graph of possible transitions and asks, which of these continuations actually survive?

20:48.780 --> 20:49.260
Right.

20:49.680 --> 20:52.920
It mathematically trims away the dead ends, the loops, and the traps.

20:53.280 --> 20:57.540
It mercilessly deletes any path that leads to a collapse in future options.

20:57.800 --> 20:58.200
Wow.

20:58.340 --> 21:02.100
It only passes forward the routes that maintain a sufficient volume of future choices.

21:02.240 --> 21:04.520
And then stage four is trajectory formation.

21:04.860 --> 21:05.160
Okay.

21:05.400 --> 21:07.960
Which tracks perfectly with our ontological inversion, right?

21:07.980 --> 21:08.100
Yeah.

21:08.460 --> 21:13.180
It stops looking at individual dots on the map and starts looking at the continuous lines connecting them.

21:13.320 --> 21:16.120
It forms sweeping paths through the filtered graph.

21:16.120 --> 21:16.420
Right.

21:16.600 --> 21:22.340
And stage five is memory, which we will dedicate an entire discussion to shortly because it redefines the concept entirely.

21:22.400 --> 21:23.000
Yes, definitely.

21:23.000 --> 21:30.860
But for now, just know that at stage five, HYDRA asks, which of these sweeping paths have persisted historically?

21:31.580 --> 21:36.840
It weights the projectories based on deep historical resonance rather than just immediate utility.

21:37.060 --> 21:37.480
Got it.

21:37.480 --> 21:41.140
And finally, stage six is RSVP coupling.

21:41.900 --> 21:51.400
The system selects the trajectory not because it's the most likely prediction of the future, but because it actively navigates the agent into the best possible future geometry.

21:51.620 --> 21:51.900
Yes.

21:52.260 --> 21:56.200
It couples the agent's actions with the structural integrity of the future.

21:56.200 --> 22:05.680
But wait, if HYDRA is actively filtering for the best future geometry in that final stage, how does a computer mathematically measure a future that hasn't even happened yet?

22:05.760 --> 22:07.000
That is the big question.

22:07.240 --> 22:09.520
You can't just tell silicon to keep its options open.

22:09.620 --> 22:10.920
It needs a hard metric, a number.

22:11.100 --> 22:11.420
Right.

22:11.600 --> 22:14.580
This takes us to the deepest mathematical layer of flictions theory.

22:15.280 --> 22:20.020
We need to talk about my absolute favorite concept in this entire monograph, the lamphron.

22:20.080 --> 22:20.840
Oh, the lamphron.

22:20.940 --> 22:21.200
Yes.

22:21.200 --> 22:24.560
The mathematics of this framework are built on four primitive objects.

22:24.920 --> 22:29.180
Think of these as the foundational laws of physics for this new computational universe.

22:29.440 --> 22:31.340
First, the trajectory space.

22:31.700 --> 22:33.500
This isn't just the space of things that are happening.

22:33.640 --> 22:40.140
It is the absolute, unfathomably massive universe of all possible histories and all possible futures.

22:40.320 --> 22:40.640
Huge.

22:40.880 --> 22:41.080
Yes.

22:41.700 --> 22:43.200
Second, the projection system.

22:43.200 --> 22:54.060
Because the trajectory space is infinite, the system needs a mathematical mechanism to compress it into something a processor can actually handle, like those stages of HYDRA we just discussed.

22:54.700 --> 23:01.180
Third is the admissibility structure, which acts as the rule set determining which moves are valid in any given context.

23:01.340 --> 23:03.440
And fourth is the persistence functional.

23:03.660 --> 23:04.500
And that's the measurement.

23:04.840 --> 23:05.140
Exactly.

23:05.140 --> 23:11.260
This is the core mathematical measurement of how successfully the system is preserving its future options.

23:11.260 --> 23:16.060
And out of that persistence functional emerges this metric called the lamphron.

23:16.400 --> 23:16.680
Yes.

23:17.040 --> 23:20.560
I want to spend some time here because this is the number that rules this new universe.

23:21.240 --> 23:22.960
What exactly is a lamphron?

23:23.240 --> 23:27.560
Mathematically, the lamphron is defined as the log volume of admissible futures.

23:27.760 --> 23:29.180
Let's ground that in reality.

23:29.780 --> 23:34.120
Conceptually, it is the exact quantifiable measurement of your potential.

23:34.620 --> 23:40.660
A state with high lamphron has a massive, sprawling volume of viable, meaningful continuations.

23:40.660 --> 23:41.620
Lots of open doors.

23:41.820 --> 23:42.240
Exactly.

23:43.120 --> 23:47.800
It represents a state of high freedom, deep adaptability, and robust resilience.

23:48.680 --> 23:52.440
Conversely, a state with low lamphron is incredibly brittle.

23:52.700 --> 23:54.320
It is a mathematical trap.

23:54.540 --> 23:54.660
Right.

23:55.080 --> 23:58.960
And Flyxion's theory dictates that true intelligence doesn't just calculate predictions.

23:58.960 --> 24:01.340
It operates on lamphrodyne flow.

24:01.480 --> 24:02.120
Lamp-ordine flow.

24:02.120 --> 24:07.940
Just as water naturally flows downhill under gravity, an intelligence system naturally flows

24:07.940 --> 24:09.720
towards states of higher lamphron.

24:10.280 --> 24:13.080
It inherently seeks greater future admissibility.

24:13.320 --> 24:16.560
Let me stop you there because this sounds completely counterintuitive when you apply it

24:16.560 --> 24:17.240
to the real world.

24:17.400 --> 24:17.860
How so?

24:18.220 --> 24:19.820
I read this section and pushed back hard.

24:19.920 --> 24:20.140
Yeah.

24:20.500 --> 24:25.960
If high lamphron maintaining the absolute maximum number of future options is the ultimate

24:25.960 --> 24:30.720
goal of an intelligent agent, wouldn't that agent just refuse to make any decisions ever?

24:30.720 --> 24:31.960
Ah, I see what you mean.

24:32.260 --> 24:37.820
Like, think about a college student who refuses to declare a major because they want to keep

24:37.820 --> 24:39.320
all their career options open.

24:39.740 --> 24:45.680
Or someone who refuses to commit to a relationship because doing so would close off other romantic

24:45.680 --> 24:46.420
possibilities.

24:46.580 --> 24:46.820
Right.

24:46.940 --> 24:48.200
The paradox of choice.

24:48.320 --> 24:48.720
Exactly.

24:48.980 --> 24:52.480
If I never commit, my lamphron stays theoretically huge, right?

24:52.500 --> 24:52.640
Yeah.

24:52.840 --> 24:55.780
But in reality, people who refuse to commit are usually paralyzed.

24:56.320 --> 24:57.180
They achieve nothing.

24:57.180 --> 25:01.760
So how does the math actually account for the necessity of making hard commitments?

25:02.100 --> 25:07.140
That is the exact paradox Flyxion addresses in a section called the geometry of intelligence.

25:07.660 --> 25:07.700
Okay.

25:08.000 --> 25:12.780
You are absolutely right that simply hovering an infinite unstructured possibility is not

25:12.780 --> 25:13.340
intelligence.

25:13.540 --> 25:14.260
It is paralysis.

25:15.160 --> 25:20.300
Flyxion mathematically defines intelligence not as the avoidance of choice, but as curvature

25:20.300 --> 25:20.920
control.

25:21.280 --> 25:21.840
Curvature control.

25:21.960 --> 25:22.100
Okay.

25:22.160 --> 25:22.900
Break that down for me.

25:22.900 --> 25:27.520
So in topology, a geometric space can have positive or negative curvature.

25:28.280 --> 25:32.660
In Flyxion's framework, negative admissibility curvature represents a trap.

25:32.860 --> 25:37.780
It is an irreversible commitment where your future options permanently collapse and you

25:37.780 --> 25:39.160
can never regain that volume.

25:39.440 --> 25:39.980
Give me a visual.

25:40.280 --> 25:44.800
Imagine walking into a canyon where the rock walls keep getting narrower and steeper and

25:44.800 --> 25:46.220
eventually you can't even turn around.

25:46.300 --> 25:46.960
You are trapped.

25:47.240 --> 25:48.420
That is negative curvature.

25:48.540 --> 25:48.680
Right.

25:48.760 --> 25:49.960
So a low lamphron state.

25:49.960 --> 25:50.580
Precisely.

25:51.100 --> 25:55.900
Intelligence is the structural ability to navigate the projectory space while actively

25:55.900 --> 25:57.260
avoiding those canyons.

25:57.660 --> 25:58.900
But here is the brilliant part.

25:59.460 --> 26:06.740
Sometimes to reach a massive open plateau of incredibly high lamphron, a rich, complex,

26:06.980 --> 26:09.280
open future, you cannot just hover.

26:09.480 --> 26:09.580
Right.

26:09.580 --> 26:12.520
You have to physically walk through a narrow mountain pass.

26:12.640 --> 26:15.500
You have to temporarily decrease your immediate options.

26:15.680 --> 26:17.200
You have to declare a major.

26:17.500 --> 26:18.740
You have to sign a contract.

26:18.880 --> 26:19.840
You have to make a commitment.

26:20.400 --> 26:23.580
So the system willingly lowers its lamphron temporarily.

26:23.740 --> 26:24.020
Yes.

26:24.360 --> 26:28.580
The fundamental difference between paralysis and true intelligence is that the intelligent

26:28.580 --> 26:34.900
system accepts a temporary localized narrowing of options if and only if it can mathematically

26:34.900 --> 26:39.980
project that the narrow pass leads to a radically broader, richer space of reachability on the

26:39.980 --> 26:40.380
other side.

26:40.740 --> 26:42.380
It doesn't avoid making choices.

26:42.820 --> 26:46.360
It avoids choices that have permanent, inescapable negative curvature.

26:47.020 --> 26:52.740
It trades a small sacrifice in immediate options for an exponential gain in future continuations.

26:52.740 --> 26:55.900
That is such a powerful way to frame decision making.

26:56.620 --> 27:01.520
You commit to the narrow path of medical school not to close your options, but because surviving

27:01.520 --> 27:07.160
that narrow pass opens up a high lamphron plateau of being a surgeon, which is totally

27:07.160 --> 27:08.140
insensible otherwise.

27:08.520 --> 27:09.040
Exactly.

27:09.420 --> 27:14.040
And the way a system calculates this curvature involves the RSVP field you mentioned during

27:14.040 --> 27:15.260
the HYDRA stages.

27:15.800 --> 27:19.020
Can we actually look at the math of the RSVP field?

27:19.200 --> 27:19.920
What are the variables?

27:19.920 --> 27:24.760
The RSVP field, the relativistic scalar vector plenum, is a field triple.

27:25.300 --> 27:29.780
It consists of three mathematically coupled variables that behave almost like fluid dynamics.

27:29.920 --> 27:30.940
Okay, fluid dynamics.

27:31.080 --> 27:31.780
First is phi.

27:32.160 --> 27:34.180
This is the scalar accessibility potential.

27:34.400 --> 27:37.900
Think of it as a heat map of the environment, telling the system where it can move with the

27:37.900 --> 27:39.860
least resistance and the highest accessibility.

27:40.440 --> 27:41.920
Okay, so phi is the map.

27:42.160 --> 27:42.400
Right.

27:42.780 --> 27:43.460
Second is nu.

27:43.640 --> 27:44.920
This is the vector flow field.

27:45.360 --> 27:47.560
If phi is the map, nu is the current.

27:47.560 --> 27:52.520
It encodes the precise direction and momentum of the system's motion through the space.

27:52.980 --> 27:53.600
And current.

27:53.840 --> 27:59.700
And third is S. This is entropic accessibility, which measures the raw volume of futures available

27:59.700 --> 28:01.020
at any given coordinate.

28:01.360 --> 28:02.240
And how do they interact?

28:02.580 --> 28:08.440
The math proves that if the flow field, nu, diverges, if the agent's energy and attention

28:08.440 --> 28:14.000
scatter in too many directions at once, it radically increases the entropy S and destroys

28:14.000 --> 28:14.560
future admissibility.

28:14.560 --> 28:17.000
So scattering your focus is bad.

28:17.180 --> 28:17.380
Yes.

28:17.880 --> 28:23.140
But if the flow field converges, if the agent engages in focused, stable, continuous navigation,

28:23.540 --> 28:29.120
it deepens the potential phi and actually generates new, previously inaccessible future

28:29.120 --> 28:29.820
geometries.

28:30.260 --> 28:32.500
Focused action literally creates new futures.

28:32.920 --> 28:37.760
Okay, so if the entire architecture from the HYDRA pipeline to the fluid dynamics of the

28:37.760 --> 28:43.360
RSVP field is obsessed with navigating toward future trajectories, where does the past fit into

28:43.360 --> 28:44.840
this?

28:44.840 --> 28:48.700
We mentioned memory briefly, but if we aren't storing static state snapshots, what physically

28:48.700 --> 28:50.020
is a memory in this system?

28:50.400 --> 28:55.040
Okay, so the traditional computer science view of memory is the filing cabinet model.

28:55.180 --> 28:55.400
Right.

28:55.700 --> 29:01.040
You experience a drink of vent, you compress it into a file, you shove it into an index

29:01.040 --> 29:05.760
drawer on a hard drive, and when you need it later, you query the index and pull the file

29:05.760 --> 29:06.260
back out.

29:06.260 --> 29:08.340
And the file just sits inert until you need it.

29:08.580 --> 29:09.060
Exactly.

29:09.480 --> 29:09.640
Yeah.

29:09.980 --> 29:13.980
Flyxion's mem-framework utterly destroys the filing cabinet model.

29:14.560 --> 29:16.560
Memory is not discrete storage.

29:17.140 --> 29:21.260
Memory is, mathematically, stabilized field residue.

29:22.220 --> 29:23.420
Stabilized field residue.

29:23.420 --> 29:25.080
I love the physical analogy for this.

29:25.580 --> 29:28.540
In this framework, a memory isn't a book placed on a shelf.

29:28.920 --> 29:31.780
A memory is a groove worn into a dirt path.

29:31.780 --> 29:34.080
That is the exact mechanical translation.

29:34.340 --> 29:34.420
Right.

29:34.480 --> 29:39.220
When a trajectory navigates through the RSVP field, when an agent takes an action or processes

29:39.220 --> 29:42.240
a thought, it leaves a physical mathematical trace in the field.

29:42.460 --> 29:47.460
It literally deepens the gradient of the phi variable along that specific route, and it

29:47.460 --> 29:49.480
locally decreases the entropy, x.

29:49.480 --> 29:50.480
It changes the space.

29:51.000 --> 29:56.000
It physically alters the topology of the space, making that exact path slightly easier to walk

29:56.000 --> 29:56.900
down the next time.

29:56.900 --> 30:02.200
So, if I walk through a dense forest, I inevitably crush some grass and snap some twigs.

30:03.040 --> 30:07.300
The next day, I stand at the edge of the woods, and I see that slightly crushed grass.

30:07.580 --> 30:10.520
So, I walk there again because it offers the path of least resistance.

30:10.700 --> 30:11.180
Exactly.

30:11.600 --> 30:12.580
I crush it a bit more.

30:13.220 --> 30:19.180
Over five years of walking that exact same route every day, it becomes a deep, hard-packed

30:19.180 --> 30:19.760
dirt trail.

30:20.300 --> 30:22.720
I never stored a map of the trail in my pocket.

30:22.720 --> 30:27.800
The environment itself retained the physical residue of my historical trajectory.

30:28.200 --> 30:33.380
And this seemingly simple shift changes everything about how we engineer retrieval systems.

30:33.520 --> 30:33.900
How so?

30:34.200 --> 30:38.480
In a von Neumann computer, saving a file to the disk and opening a file from the disk are

30:38.480 --> 30:41.220
two entirely separate, unrelated operations.

30:41.640 --> 30:44.340
But in MEM 8, retrieval is reinforcement.

30:44.620 --> 30:44.860
Wow.

30:45.060 --> 30:49.340
The act of remembering is physically indistinguishable from the act of deepening the groove.

30:49.340 --> 30:54.440
When you send a signal down a past path, you strengthen the attractor basin of that path.

30:54.640 --> 30:56.060
Which means forgetting isn't a failure.

30:56.260 --> 30:56.520
Exactly.

30:56.740 --> 31:00.660
In a filing cabinet, a lost file is a catastrophic glitch in the hardware.

31:01.420 --> 31:08.340
But in MEM 8, forgetting is the intended natural thermodynamic dissipation of the RSVP field.

31:08.620 --> 31:09.720
The grass grows back.

31:09.840 --> 31:10.180
Yes.

31:10.720 --> 31:15.520
If a path isn't walked for a long time, the physical environment naturally smooths out.

31:15.520 --> 31:18.480
The grass slowly grows back over the dirt trail.

31:18.900 --> 31:19.800
The entropy returns.

31:20.540 --> 31:24.940
Forgetting is how the system maintains its flexibility and avoids getting permanently stuck

31:24.940 --> 31:26.040
in obsolete ruts.

31:26.300 --> 31:32.160
This leads to a fascinating philosophical conclusion that the text calls the history-native principle,

31:32.680 --> 31:34.940
backed up by the replay-invariance theorem.

31:35.080 --> 31:35.380
Yes.

31:35.820 --> 31:40.760
If memory is just the accumulated groove of the history we've walked, then the sequence of

31:40.760 --> 31:44.660
events, the history, is the most primitive, fundamental truth in the universe.

31:44.660 --> 31:45.140
That's right.

31:45.400 --> 31:49.140
The theorem proves mathematically that the present state is nothing more than a derived

31:49.140 --> 31:50.740
shadow cast by the history.

31:50.840 --> 31:51.220
A shadow.

31:51.300 --> 31:55.520
You can take a rich, complex history and play it forward to calculate what the current state

31:55.520 --> 31:56.060
must be.

31:56.360 --> 32:01.380
But, and this is critical, you cannot look at a current state and perfectly reverse-engineer

32:01.380 --> 32:01.900
the history.

32:02.040 --> 32:02.240
Why?

32:02.360 --> 32:08.600
Because multiple, totally different historical trajectories could have easily worn the exact

32:08.600 --> 32:10.720
same superficial groove into the dirt.

32:10.720 --> 32:13.740
The history contains the fiber of reality.

32:14.600 --> 32:16.700
The present state is just a projection.

32:16.960 --> 32:17.060
Okay.

32:17.180 --> 32:20.480
I am with you on the dirt paths, the shadows, and the history.

32:21.180 --> 32:26.080
But now I have to demand a translation because later in this section, Flyxion dives into a

32:26.080 --> 32:28.940
concept called sheaf theoretic semantics.

32:29.200 --> 32:29.760
Oh, boy.

32:30.260 --> 32:34.020
And something mathematically terrifying called a kuhomological obstruction.

32:34.700 --> 32:36.440
My brain melted reading this.

32:36.540 --> 32:37.960
What does this actually mean in practice?

32:37.960 --> 32:42.840
Huh, I know, it sounds incredibly intimidating, but it is arguably one of the most practical,

32:43.040 --> 32:45.240
mind-blowing insights in the entire monograph.

32:46.060 --> 32:51.420
That dense math is actually the exact mathematical proof for why AI chatbots hallucinate.

32:51.520 --> 32:51.920
Wait, really?

32:52.300 --> 32:56.760
